ORGO.reg({"types":{"williamson-why":{"id":"williamson-why","title":"Why one ether synthesis works and the other fails","exam":"Q5A","points":80,"asks":"Two ways of joining the same two pieces are tried. One gives the ether, the other does not. Explain with structures and mechanisms.","know":[{"h":"<b>The Williamson ether synthesis is an {{SN2}} reaction.</b> An alkoxide ({{RO-}}) attacks the carbon that carries a leaving group from the back, and the leaving group leaves in the same step: two arrows, no intermediate.","from":"Lecture 11/05, p. 1 (reaction C); FA24 Exam 2, Q5A"},{"h":"<b>Every unsymmetrical ether can be cut two ways.</b> For R-O-R', the oxygen can arrive on R ({{RO-}} with R'-X) or on R' ({{R'O-}} with R-X). The pieces are the same; only the piece that carries the leaving group changes.","from":"standard"},{"h":"<b>The carbon that carries the leaving group decides.</b> {{SN2}} rates: methyl 1, ethyl (primary) 0.033, isopropyl (secondary) 0.00083, tert-butyl (tertiary) 0. Tertiary halides never do {{SN2}}.","from":"Lecture 11/10, Figure 7.3"},{"h":"<b>Alkoxides are strong bases.</b> Their conjugate acids are alcohols: methanol {{pKa}} 15.5, ethanol 15.9, isopropanol 16.5, tert-butanol 17.0. Where {{SN2}} is slow, a strong base eliminates instead.","from":"Lecture 10/29, Table 6.8"},{"h":"<b>Strong base with a tertiary halide gives {{E2}} only.</b> In lecture, tert-butyl bromide with NaOH gave 2-methylpropene and no alcohol: rate = k[RBr][base].","from":"Lecture 11/12, p. 1; flowchart"},{"h":"<b>Strong base with a secondary halide gives mostly {{E2}}.</b> 2-Bromopropane with ethoxide gives mostly propene and only a little ether.","from":"FA24 Exam 2, Q7A; flowchart"},{"h":"<b>The {{E2}} mechanism has three arrows</b>: base lone pair to a beta hydrogen, the C-H bond to the new C=C, the C-X bond to X. The hydrogen and the leaving group are anti-periplanar. The products are the alkene, the conjugate acid of the base (an alcohol) and the halide ion.","from":"Lecture 11/12, p. 1 to 3"},{"h":"<b>A methyl halide can only substitute.</b> It has no beta hydrogen, so {{E2}} is impossible, and a methyl cation never forms, so there is no {{SN1}} or {{E1}}. A benzyl halide also has no beta hydrogen on an {{sp3}} carbon.","from":"flowchart"},{"h":"<b>Bulk hurts far more on the halide than on the alkoxide.</b> tert-Butoxide is a poor nucleophile, yet it still attacks the open carbon of {{CH3I}} and gives tert-butyl methyl ether.","from":"Lecture 11/05, p. 1 and 3; FA24 Exam 2, Q5A"},{"h":"<b>A bulky strong base with a primary halide that has beta hydrogens is sent to {{E2}} by the flowchart.</b> So the safest partner for a bulky alkoxide is a methyl halide.","from":"flowchart"},{"h":"<b>No {{SN2}} and no {{SN1}} at an {{sp2}} carbon</b> (vinyl or aryl: rate 0). An aryl ether must be made from the phenoxide ({{ArO-}}) and an alkyl halide. Phenol has {{pKa}} 10, so its anion is easy to make and is a weak base.","from":"Lecture 11/10, Figure 7.3; FA24 Exam 2, Q2A and Q1A"},{"h":"<b>Two alcohols do not make an ether this way.</b> A neutral alcohol is a poor nucleophile ({{CH3OH}} 1 against {{HO-}} 16,000) and {{HO-}} is a strong base and a poor leaving group.","from":"Lecture 11/05, p. 2, Table 7.5"},{"h":"<b>Skills:</b> draw the {{SN2}} step with two arrows, draw the {{E2}} step with three arrows, and write the products the failed route really gives. A stereocentre on the alkoxide carbon is untouched, because no bond to it is made or broken.","from":"FA24 Exam 2, Q5A rubric"}],"method":["Cut the ether on each side of the oxygen and write the two alkoxide and halide pairs.","In each pair, classify the carbon that carries the leaving group: methyl, primary, secondary, tertiary or {{sp2}}.","Pick the pair whose halide is methyl or primary, best of all one with no beta hydrogen. The branched or ring piece goes in the alkoxide.","For the pair that works, draw the {{SN2}} step: oxygen lone pair to carbon from the back, C-X bond to X, and the ether as product.","For the pair that fails, say why {{SN2}} cannot happen (three alkyl groups block the back of a tertiary carbon, or the carbon is {{sp2}}).","Then say what happens instead: an alkoxide is a strong base, so draw the {{E2}} step with three arrows and write the alkene, the alcohol and the halide ion. If the carbon is {{sp2}}, write no reaction.","Finish with the lesson in one line: methyl or primary halide, bulky group in the alkoxide."],"pitfalls":[{"h":"Judging the route by the bulk of the alkoxide. The class of the halide carbon decides: tert-butoxide with {{CH3I}} works, methoxide with a tertiary halide does not."},{"h":"Writing \"no reaction\" for an alkoxide with a tertiary halide. {{E2}} does happen: draw the alkene, the alcohol and the halide ion."},{"h":"Calling the failed route {{SN1}} or {{E1}}. A strong base is present, so it removes a beta hydrogen in one step before the halide can ionize."},{"h":"Drawing the {{E2}} arrow from the base to carbon, or leaving out the conjugate acid of the base (methanol from methoxide) in the products."},{"h":"Explaining only the reaction that works. Most of the points are for why the other fails and what it gives."},{"h":"Inverting a stereocentre that belongs to the alkoxide. Inversion happens at the carbon that is attacked, which is on the halide."}],"links":{"read":["choose","sn2-2","e2"]}}},"items":{"exam-q5a-written":{"id":"exam-q5a-written","type":"williamson-why","role":"exam","kind":"written","diff":2,"src":"FA24 Exam 2, Q5A","time":12,"pts":80,"stem":"tert-Butyl methyl ether forms in the first reaction but not in the second. Explain why, and draw the mechanism of what happens in each flask.\n[[rxn:CC(C)(C)[O-] + CI>>CC(C)(C)OC]]\n[[nr:C[O-] + CC(C)(C)I>>CC(C)(C)OC]]","model":"<p><b>The first reaction is {{SN2}}.</b> The electrophile is {{CH3I}}: a methyl carbon, the least hindered there is, with no beta hydrogen. tert-Butoxide attacks it from the back and iodide leaves. Elimination is impossible, so the ether is the only product.</p>\n[[mech:sn2-tertbutoxide-iodomethane]]\n<p><b>The second reaction cannot be {{SN2}}.</b> The electrophile is now a <b>tertiary</b> iodide. Three methyl groups block the back side of the carbon, so methoxide cannot attack it.</p><p><b>Methoxide is also a strong base</b>, so it removes a beta hydrogen instead: <b>{{E2}}</b>. Three arrows: O lone pair to H, C-H bond to the new C=C, C-I bond to I.</p>\n[[mech:e2-tertbutyl-iodide-methoxide]]\n<p>The products of the second flask are 2-methylpropene, methanol and iodide. No ether.</p><p>The lesson for making an ether this way: let the halide be methyl or primary, and put the bulky group in the alkoxide.</p>","rubric":[{"pts":20,"h":"SN2 mechanism for the reaction that works: O lone pair to the methyl carbon, C-I bond to I, ether drawn"},{"pts":15,"h":"Says the tertiary iodide cannot undergo SN2 because of steric hindrance at the electrophilic carbon"},{"pts":10,"h":"Says methoxide is a strong base"},{"pts":25,"h":"E2 mechanism with three correct arrows: O lone pair to a beta H, C-H bond to the C=C, C-I bond to I"},{"pts":10,"h":"Names or draws what actually forms: 2-methylpropene, methanol and iodide"}],"solution":{"steps":[{"t":"Look at the electrophile, not the nucleophile","h":"Both flasks contain an alkoxide and an alkyl iodide, and both would give the same ether if substitution happened. What differs is which partner carries the leaving group: a methyl carbon in the first, a tertiary carbon in the second."},{"t":"First flask","h":"Methyl iodide is the best SN2 substrate (relative rate 1; tert-butyl is 0). It has no beta hydrogen, so even a bulky strong base like tert-butoxide can only substitute. The lecture drew this very reaction on 11/05."},{"t":"Second flask: why not SN2","h":"SN2 needs the nucleophile at the back of the carbon. On a tertiary carbon three methyl groups are in the way. The size of the nucleophile is not the problem here: methoxide is small."},{"t":"Second flask: what happens instead","h":"Methoxide is a strong base, and tert-butyl iodide has nine beta hydrogens on the outside of the molecule. Strong base plus tertiary halide is E2 on the flowchart."},{"t":"Write the real products","h":"2-Methylpropene, {{CH3OH}} and {{I-}}. Say plainly that no ether forms."}],"answer":"First: SN2 at an unhindered methyl carbon gives the ether. Second: SN2 is blocked at the tertiary carbon, and methoxide, a strong base, does E2 instead to give 2-methylpropene, methanol and iodide.","rule":"Ether by SN2: the halide must be methyl or primary. The bulky group goes in the alkoxide."}},"exam-q5a-sn2":{"id":"exam-q5a-sn2","type":"williamson-why","role":"exam","kind":"mech","diff":1,"src":"FA24 Exam 2, Q5A","mech":"sn2-tertbutoxide-iodomethane","stem":"Draw the curved arrows for the reaction that works: tert-butoxide with iodomethane.","solution":{"steps":[{"t":"Decide the mechanism","h":"Methyl halide: SN2 is the only possibility. No carbocation, no beta hydrogen."},{"t":"Draw the two arrows","h":"A lone pair on the alkoxide oxygen to the methyl carbon. The C-I bond to iodine."}],"answer":"One step, two arrows: tert-butyl methyl ether and iodide.","rule":"SN2: nucleophile in from the back, leaving group out."}},"exam-q5a-e2":{"id":"exam-q5a-e2","type":"williamson-why","role":"exam","kind":"mech","diff":2,"src":"FA24 Exam 2, Q5A","mech":"e2-tertbutyl-iodide-methoxide","stem":"Draw the curved arrows for what really happens when methoxide meets tert-butyl iodide.","solution":{"steps":[{"t":"Decide the mechanism","h":"Tertiary halide, strong base: E2. Substitution at this carbon is blocked."},{"t":"Draw the three arrows","h":"Methoxide lone pair to a hydrogen on one of the methyl groups. That C-H bond to the bond between the methyl carbon and the central carbon. The C-I bond to iodine."}],"answer":"One step, three arrows: 2-methylpropene, methanol and iodide.","rule":"E2: base to H, C-H to the pi bond, C-LG to the leaving group."}}},"mols":{"CC(C)(C)[O-]":{"w":64,"h":68,"p":[[0,"M 7.8,49.1 L 31.7,35.3",0],[1,"M 31.7,35.3 L 55.5,21.5",0],[2,"M 31.7,35.3 L 45.4,59.2",0],[3,"M 31.7,35.3 L 21.6,17.9",0]],"t":[[4,14.6,17,11,"O"],[4,22.2,12.6,7,"-"]],"a":[[7.8,49.1,"C",0,0,3,0],[31.7,35.3,"C",0,0,0,0],[55.5,21.5,"C",0,0,3,0],[45.4,59.2,"C",0,0,3,0],[17.9,11.5,"O",-1,3,0,0]],"b":[[0,1,1],[1,2,1],[1,3,1],[1,4,1]]},"CI":{"w":41,"h":11,"p":[[0,"M 6.4,4.3 L 27.6,4.3",0]],"t":[[1,28.4,9.3,10,"I"]],"a":[[6.4,4.3,"C",0,0,3,0],[31.4,4.3,"I",0,3,0,0]],"b":[[0,1,1]]},"CC(C)(C)OC":{"w":96,"h":64,"p":[[0,"M 21.7,7.7 L 35.5,31.7",0],[1,"M 35.5,31.7 L 49.4,55.7",0],[2,"M 35.5,31.7 L 11.5,45.5",0],[3,"M 35.5,31.7 L 55.3,20.3",0],[4,"M 63.8,20.3 L 83.5,31.7",0]],"t":[[4,56.2,23.4,11,"O"]],"a":[[21.7,7.7,"C",0,0,3,0],[35.5,31.7,"C",0,0,0,0],[49.4,55.7,"C",0,0,3,0],[11.5,45.5,"C",0,0,3,0],[59.5,17.8,"O",0,2,0,0],[83.5,31.7,"C",0,0,3,0]],"b":[[0,1,1],[1,2,1],[1,3,1],[1,4,1],[4,5,1]]},"C[O-]":{"w":45,"h":11,"p":[[0,"M 7.1,4.3 L 28.3,4.3",0]],"t":[[1,29.1,9.3,10,"O"],[1,36,5.3,6,"-"]],"a":[[7.1,4.3,"C",0,0,3,0],[32.1,4.3,"O",-1,3,0,0]],"b":[[0,1,1]]},"CC(C)(C)I":{"w":64,"h":68,"p":[[0,"M 7.8,49.1 L 31.7,35.3",0],[1,"M 31.7,35.3 L 55.5,21.5",0],[2,"M 31.7,35.3 L 45.4,59.2",0],[3,"M 31.7,35.3 L 21.6,17.9",0]],"t":[[4,14.6,17,11,"I"]],"a":[[7.8,49.1,"C",0,0,3,0],[31.7,35.3,"C",0,0,0,0],[55.5,21.5,"C",0,0,3,0],[45.4,59.2,"C",0,0,3,0],[17.9,11.5,"I",0,3,0,0]],"b":[[0,1,1],[1,2,1],[1,3,1],[1,4,1]]}}});
